{"assignees":[],"author":{"id":"MDQ6VXNlcjIwOTkwODQ2","is_bot":false,"login":"ehvidal","name":"Esteban Vidal"},"body":"**Extension:** `@webframp/redmine` 2026.10.14.1

**What I wanted:** read the files attached to an issue (for example, screenshots in a
journal note) from a workflow.

**What happens:** there is no way to. `get_issue` does not request `include=attachments`,
and `upload_file` is the only file method. A method extension cannot fill the gap: it can
add `resources`, but not `files`, so storing the content fails with
`Undeclared file spec 'attachment' in behavior '@webframp/redmine'. Declared file specs: (none)`.
The file spec has to be in the base type.

**Suggested fix** (patch below, 599 lines with tests; use any of it, attribution not needed):

- `list_attachments(issueId)`: an `attachments` resource named `attachments-<issueId>`
  (ID, file name, size, content type, author, date).
- `download_attachments(issueId, attachmentIds?, contentTypePrefix?, maxBytes = 20 MiB)`:
  one run stores each file in a new `attachment` file spec, named
  `attachment-<issueId>-<attachmentId>`, and writes the list with each file's `dataName`
  and `status` (`downloaded` / `skipped-too-large`).
- `_lib/api.ts`: `redmineDownload`, which reuses `fetchWithRetry` and sends
  `X-Redmine-Username` like every other call.
- The names are prefixed by spec on purpose. Data names are shared across specs, so
  writing the list as `<issueId>` would interleave versions with `issue_detail`.
- Version 2026.10.20.1, identity upgrade entry, release notes. 54 tests pass, quality 100%.

We have run it from source against a real Redmine (7 attachments, all downloaded).

<details>
<summary>Patch (against main at ae220f8b)</summary>

```diff
